---
import ScrollLayout from "../../layouts/ScrollLayout.astro";
import LinkCard from "../../components/LinkCard.astro";

const results = [
  { value: "−22%", label: "exposure error vs syncing the key photos" },
  { value: "−12%", label: "white balance error vs syncing the key photos" },
  { value: "0.75", label: "mean crop overlap (IoU) with my own crops; 0.66 for my average crop" },
  { value: "1.9%", label: "photos where it picks the wrong main dancer (down from 10.1%)" },
  { value: "0.1%", label: "crops that cut into the couple (down from 36.5%)" },
  { value: "0.94°", label: "straightening error vs 1.29° when never rotating" },
];

const stack = [
  "Python", "LightGBM", "scikit-learn", "PyTorch", "OpenCLIP", "Ultralytics YOLO11-pose",
  "GeoCalib", "OpenCV", "pandas", "SQLite", "Lua (Lightroom SDK)", "Git",
];
---

<ScrollLayout
  title="Auto Photo Edit"
  description="A Lightroom Classic plugin that learned Rob's editing, cropping and straightening style from five years of swing-dance photography."
  ornament="❖"
  tagline="A Lightroom Classic plugin that learned my editing, cropping and straightening style from five years of my own swing-dance photography, and now handles the repetitive part of every event."
  introBox
  showBackLink
>
  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The Enchantment</h2>
    <p>
      I photograph couples dancing at swing socials: 100–200 keepers per event, each corrected by hand
      for white balance, exposure, highlights and shadows, dehaze, sharpening, noise reduction and
      vignette, then cropped and straightened. Roughly eight hours of editing per social.
    </p>
    <p>Now the workflow is:</p>
    <ol class="ritual">
      <li>Edit two or three key photos from the event by hand and give them a green label.</li>
      <li>Select the whole event and run <em>Auto Edit + Crop</em>.</li>
      <li>
        The plugin syncs my look to every photo, corrects exposure and white balance per photo, crops
        each one around the dancing couple the way I frame them, and straightens it.
      </li>
      <li>I review and fine-tune. Everything is ordinary Lightroom settings, undone in one step.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>How the Spell Was Learned</h2>
    <ul class="runes">
      <li>
        <strong>Mined the catalog.</strong> Lightroom's catalog is a SQLite database; I parsed the
        Lua-serialised develop settings of 11,779 edited raw photos into a training set: the original
        photo plus exactly what I did to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recovered the "before" images.</strong> A minimal DNG/TIFF reader seeks straight to the
        embedded preview and the camera's as-shot white balance instead of decoding 20&nbsp;MB raws,
        taking extraction from about 50 to 2,600 files a minute.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tone model.</strong> Image statistics, EXIF and CLIP scene embeddings feed gradient-boosted
        models trained on how each photo differs from the rest of its event.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Crop model.</strong> YOLO11 pose detection finds every person and their body points; a
        subject classifier, trained on 67,213 people labelled by whether I kept them in frame, tells the
        couple from blurry bystanders; a regression model predicts my framing relative to the couple,
        and a constraint guarantees heads and feet stay in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Straightening.</strong> A line-detection approach was tested and rejected (it rotated the
        wrong way a quarter of the time). GeoCalib's gravity estimate, calibrated against my own past
        rotations, made the cut.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The plugin.</strong> A Lua plugin gathers the selection and hands it to a local Python
        process, then applies the predicted develop settings in a single undoable step. Key photos and
        existing crops are never touched.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Trials &amp; Proofs</h2>
    <p>
      Every number below comes from events the models never saw during training (grouped
      cross-validation), measured against simple baselines. Ideas that didn't beat the baseline,
      like a fully automatic "look" or line-based straightening, didn't ship.
    </p>
    <ul class="stats">
      {results.map((r) => (
        <li><b>{r.value}</b><span>{r.label}</span></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Arcane Architecture</h2>
    <figure class="diagram">
      <a href="/auto-photo-edit/architecture.png">
        <img src="/auto-photo-edit/architecture.png" width="2080" height="1400" loading="lazy"
          alt="Architecture: the Lightroom Lua plugin sends a request to a local Python inference process (load and describe, tone settings, crop settings, straighten), which uses models produced by an offline training pipeline that reads a copy of the Lightroom catalog." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>Lightroom plugin, local inference and the offline training pipeline. Select to enlarge.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Wards of Privacy</h2>
    <ul class="runes">
      <li><strong>Runs locally.</strong> Training and editing run on my own PC; the plugin never uploads photos.</li>
      <li><strong>No generative AI on the photos.</strong> It only sets Lightroom's own sliders, crop and angle: faces and bodies are never altered.</li>
      <li><strong>A human casts the final spell.</strong> I review every photo before anything is shared.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Instruments</h2>
    <ul class="stack">
      {stack.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">Developed with AI pair-programming (Claude Code).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Field Poster</h2>
    <figure class="diagram">
      <a href="/auto-photo-edit/infographic.png">
        <img src="/auto-photo-edit/infographic.png" width="1600" height="2440" loading="lazy"
          alt="Infographic summarising how Auto Photo Edit learns from the catalog, how it is used in Lightroom, what happens to each photo, results, and privacy." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>The whole project on one page. Select to enlarge.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Known Tomes &amp; Portals</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <LinkCard
          href="https://github.com/Roblosapiens/auto-photo-edit"
          title="auto-photo-edit on GitHub"
          description="Source code for the plugin, training pipeline and evaluation, plus a guide to building your own from your catalog."
          external
        />
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</ScrollLayout>
